{"subjectId":"dsa-advanced","id":"queue_deque","title":"Queue & Deque","group":"Linked Lists, Stacks & Queues","order":12,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A queue is <strong>First In, First Out</strong>; a deque (<em>double-ended queue</em>) lets you insert and remove at <strong>both</strong> ends in <code>O(1)</code>. The advanced problems here use the queue for breadth-first generation and the deque as a <em>sliding window</em> that keeps only the candidates that can still matter.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Deque</strong> — a linear container with <code>O(1)</code> <code>pushFront</code>, <code>pushBack</code>, <code>popFront</code>, <code>popBack</code>, <code>front</code> and <code>back</code>. Restricting it to <code>pushBack</code> + <code>popFront</code> gives a queue; <code>pushBack</code> + <code>popBack</code> gives a stack — one structure covers both.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>A deque: both ends are open</figcaption><pre><code>pushFront(x) ──▶ ┌────┬────┬────┬────┬────┐ ◀── pushBack(x)\npopFront()   ◀── │ 20 │ 10 │ 15 │ 30 │ 7  │ ──▶ popBack()\n                 └────┴────┴────┴────┴────┘\n                  front                back</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box\"></i>Operations and the built-in containers</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Python <code>collections.deque</code></th><th>C++ <code>std::deque</code></th><th>Java <code>ArrayDeque</code></th><th>JavaScript</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>push back</td><td><code>append(x)</code></td><td><code>push_back(x)</code></td><td><code>addLast(x)</code> / <code>offer(x)</code></td><td><code>arr.push(x)</code></td></tr><tr><td>push front</td><td><code>appendleft(x)</code></td><td><code>push_front(x)</code></td><td><code>addFirst(x)</code> / <code>push(x)</code></td><td><code>arr.unshift(x)</code> — <strong>O(n)</strong></td></tr><tr><td>pop front</td><td><code>popleft()</code></td><td><code>pop_front()</code> (+ <code>front()</code>)</td><td><code>pollFirst()</code> / <code>poll()</code></td><td><code>arr.shift()</code> — <strong>O(n)</strong></td></tr><tr><td>pop back</td><td><code>pop()</code></td><td><code>pop_back()</code> (+ <code>back()</code>)</td><td><code>pollLast()</code></td><td><code>arr.pop()</code></td></tr><tr><td>peek front / back</td><td><code>dq[0]</code> / <code>dq[-1]</code></td><td><code>front()</code> / <code>back()</code></td><td><code>peekFirst()</code> / <code>peekLast()</code></td><td><code>arr[0]</code> / <code>arr[arr.length-1]</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>JavaScript has no built-in deque. <code>shift()</code> and <code>unshift()</code> move every element, so a queue built on them is <code>O(n)</code> per operation. For small inputs it does not matter (the first two problems below use it for brevity); for real workloads keep a <strong>head index</strong> into the array (<code>arr[head++]</code> to dequeue — see the sliding-window solution) or write a small ring-buffer / two-index <code>Deque</code> class (see the min/max structure).</p></div>\n<p>Python's <code>deque</code>, C++'s <code>std::deque</code> and Java's <code>ArrayDeque</code> are all <code>O(1)</code> at both ends. <code>std::queue</code> and <code>std::stack</code> are thin adapters over <code>std::deque</code>; Java's <code>Queue</code> and <code>Deque</code> interfaces are both implemented by <code>ArrayDeque</code> (prefer it over <code>LinkedList</code>, which allocates a node per element).</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-left-right\"></i>Reverse a queue</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>q = [20, 10, 15, 30] (20 at the front)</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>30 15 10 20</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>A stack reverses order. Dequeue everything into a stack, then pop everything back into the queue: the last element out of the queue is the first one pushed back. Only queue operations (<code>enqueue</code>, <code>dequeue</code>) and a stack are used — the queue is treated as a black box.</p></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from collections import deque\n\ndef reverse_queue(q):\n    st = []\n    while q:\n        st.append(q.popleft())     # drain the queue into a stack\n    while st:\n        q.append(st.pop())         # refill: last out of the queue goes in first\n\nq = deque([20, 10, 15, 30])\nreverse_queue(q)\nprint(*q)</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;queue&gt;\n#include &lt;stack&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nvoid reverseQueue(queue&lt;int&gt;&amp; q) {\n    stack&lt;int&gt; st;\n    while (!q.empty()) { st.push(q.front()); q.pop(); }   // drain the queue into a stack\n    while (!st.empty()) { q.push(st.top()); st.pop(); }   // refill: last out goes in first\n}\n\nint main() {\n    queue&lt;int&gt; q;\n    for (int x : {20, 10, 15, 30}) q.push(x);\n    reverseQueue(q);\n    while (!q.empty()) { cout &lt;&lt; q.front() &lt;&lt; \" \"; q.pop(); }\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.Deque;\nimport java.util.Queue;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static void reverseQueue(Queue&lt;Integer&gt; q) {\n        Deque&lt;Integer&gt; st = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();\n        while (!q.isEmpty()) st.push(q.poll());   // drain the queue into a stack\n        while (!st.isEmpty()) q.add(st.pop());    // refill: last out goes in first\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Queue&lt;Integer&gt; q = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();\n        for (int x : new int[]{20, 10, 15, 30}) q.add(x);\n        reverseQueue(q);\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        while (!q.isEmpty()) sb.append(q.poll()).append(' ');\n        System.out.println(sb.toString().trim());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function reverseQueue(q) {\n    const st = [];\n    while (q.length) st.push(q.shift());     // drain the queue into a stack (shift is O(n) in JS)\n    while (st.length) q.push(st.pop());      // refill: last out goes in first\n}\n\nconst q = [20, 10, 15, 30];\nreverseQueue(q);\nconsole.log(q.join(\" \"));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>30 15 10 20</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n) for the stack</span></div>\n<p><strong>Recursive version.</strong> The call stack <em>is</em> the stack: dequeue the front, reverse the rest recursively, then enqueue the front at the back. The deepest call enqueues the last element first.</p>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from collections import deque\n\ndef reverse_queue(q):\n    if not q:\n        return\n    front = q.popleft()      # take the front out …\n    reverse_queue(q)         # … reverse the remaining n-1 …\n    q.append(front)          # … and put the old front at the back\n\nq = deque([20, 10, 15, 30])\nreverse_queue(q)\nprint(*q)</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;queue&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nvoid reverseQueue(queue&lt;int&gt;&amp; q) {\n    if (q.empty()) return;\n    int front = q.front(); q.pop();   // take the front out …\n    reverseQueue(q);                  // … reverse the remaining n-1 …\n    q.push(front);                    // … and put the old front at the back\n}\n\nint main() {\n    queue&lt;int&gt; q;\n    for (int x : {20, 10, 15, 30}) q.push(x);\n    reverseQueue(q);\n    while (!q.empty()) { cout &lt;&lt; q.front() &lt;&lt; \" \"; q.pop(); }\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.Queue;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static void reverseQueue(Queue&lt;Integer&gt; q) {\n        if (q.isEmpty()) return;\n        int front = q.poll();   // take the front out …\n        reverseQueue(q);        // … reverse the remaining n-1 …\n        q.add(front);           // … and put the old front at the back\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Queue&lt;Integer&gt; q = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();\n        for (int x : new int[]{20, 10, 15, 30}) q.add(x);\n        reverseQueue(q);\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        while (!q.isEmpty()) sb.append(q.poll()).append(' ');\n        System.out.println(sb.toString().trim());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function reverseQueue(q) {\n    if (q.length === 0) return;\n    const front = q.shift();   // take the front out …\n    reverseQueue(q);           // … reverse the remaining n-1 …\n    q.push(front);             // … and put the old front at the back\n}\n\nconst q = [20, 10, 15, 30];\nreverseQueue(q);\nconsole.log(q.join(\" \"));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>30 15 10 20</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n) call stack</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>The recursive version uses <code>n</code> stack frames — Python's default limit is about 1000, so it is a teaching tool, not a production routine. The iterative version has the same complexity without the depth limit.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-numbers\"></i>Generate the first n numbers with given digits</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>n = 10, digits = [5, 6]</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>5 6 55 56 65 66 555 556 565 566</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>n = 7, digits = [1, 2, 3]</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>1 2 3 11 12 13 21</code></p></div>\n<p>Print, in increasing order, the first <code>n</code> positive integers whose decimal digits all come from the given set.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Naive — test every natural number; with digits {5, 6} only 2^k of the k-digit numbers qualify, so this gets slow fast</figcaption><pre><code>def only_digits(num, digits):\n    while num &gt; 0:\n        if num % 10 not in digits:\n            return False\n        num //= 10\n    return True\n\ndef first_n_naive(n, digits):\n    res, num = [], 1\n    while len(res) &lt; n:                 # try 1, 2, 3, … and keep the ones that qualify\n        if only_digits(num, digits):\n            res.append(num)\n        num += 1\n    return res\n\nprint(*first_n_naive(10, [5, 6]))</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>5 6 55 56 65 66 555 556 565 566</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>The valid numbers form a <strong>tree</strong>: the children of <code>x</code> are <code>x</code> with each allowed digit appended. A breadth-first traversal of that tree visits the numbers <em>in increasing order</em> — all 1-digit numbers, then all 2-digit numbers (smaller prefixes first), and so on. BFS is a queue: start with the single digits, repeatedly dequeue a number, print it, and enqueue its children. Each dequeue produces exactly one answer, so the loop runs <code>n</code> times.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>BFS order of the digit tree is numeric order, because every level is one digit longer and siblings are enqueued smallest-first</figcaption><pre><code>           (root)\n        /          \\\n       5            6                 ← dequeued 1st, 2nd\n     /   \\        /   \\\n   55     56    65     66             ← 3rd … 6th\n  /  \\   /  \\\n555 556 565 566 …                     ← 7th … 10th</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from collections import deque\n\ndef first_n(n, digits):\n    q = deque(str(d) for d in sorted(digits))   # level 1: the single digits, smallest first\n    res = []\n    while len(res) &lt; n:\n        cur = q.popleft()\n        res.append(cur)\n        for d in sorted(digits):\n            q.append(cur + str(d))              # children: one digit longer\n    return res\n\nprint(*first_n(10, [5, 6]))\nprint(*first_n(7, [1, 2, 3]))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;queue&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nvector&lt;string&gt; firstN(int n, vector&lt;int&gt; digits) {\n    sort(digits.begin(), digits.end());\n    queue&lt;string&gt; q;\n    for (int d : digits) q.push(to_string(d));     // level 1: the single digits, smallest first\n    vector&lt;string&gt; res;\n    while ((int)res.size() &lt; n) {\n        string cur = q.front(); q.pop();\n        res.push_back(cur);\n        for (int d : digits) q.push(cur + to_string(d));   // children: one digit longer\n    }\n    return res;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    for (const string&amp; s : firstN(10, {5, 6})) cout &lt;&lt; s &lt;&lt; \" \";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    for (const string&amp; s : firstN(7, {1, 2, 3})) cout &lt;&lt; s &lt;&lt; \" \";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.Arrays;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Queue;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static List&lt;String&gt; firstN(int n, int[] digits) {\n        Arrays.sort(digits);\n        Queue&lt;String&gt; q = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();\n        for (int d : digits) q.add(String.valueOf(d));   // level 1: the single digits, smallest first\n        List&lt;String&gt; res = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();\n        while (res.size() &lt; n) {\n            String cur = q.poll();\n            res.add(cur);\n            for (int d : digits) q.add(cur + d);          // children: one digit longer\n        }\n        return res;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(String.join(\" \", firstN(10, new int[]{5, 6})));\n        System.out.println(String.join(\" \", firstN(7, new int[]{1, 2, 3})));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function firstN(n, digits) {\n    digits = [...digits].sort((a, b) =&gt; a - b);\n    const q = digits.map(String);                  // level 1: the single digits, smallest first\n    const res = [];\n    while (res.length &lt; n) {\n        const cur = q.shift();\n        res.push(cur);\n        for (const d of digits) q.push(cur + d);   // children: one digit longer\n    }\n    return res;\n}\n\nconsole.log(firstN(10, [5, 6]).join(\" \"));\nconsole.log(firstN(7, [1, 2, 3]).join(\" \"));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>5 6 55 56 65 66 555 556 565 566\n1 2 3 11 12 13 21</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n × k) for k allowed digits</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n × k) queue</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Work with <strong>strings</strong>, not integers: appending a digit is string concatenation, and the numbers quickly exceed 64 bits (the 20th number with digits {5, 6} already has 5 digits; the 10 000th has 13). The original notes hard-coded <code>'5'</code> and <code>'6'</code>; the version above takes any digit set and sorts it so BFS order is numeric order.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-vertical\"></i>Data structure with min/max operations</h3>\n<p>Design a structure supporting all of the following in <code>O(1)</code>: <code>insertMin(x)</code>, <code>insertMax(x)</code>, <code>getMin()</code>, <code>getMax()</code>, <code>extractMin()</code>, <code>extractMax()</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>insertMin(5), insertMax(10), insertMin(3), insertMax(12); getMin, getMax, extractMin, getMin, extractMax, getMax</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>3 12 3 5 12 10</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p><code>insertMin(x)</code> is only ever called with a value that <strong>is</strong> the new minimum, and <code>insertMax(x)</code> only with the new maximum — that contract is what makes <code>O(1)</code> possible (a general priority queue needs <code>O(log n)</code>). So keep the elements <strong>sorted in a deque</strong>: the minimum lives at the front, the maximum at the back. <code>insertMin</code> = <code>pushFront</code>, <code>insertMax</code> = <code>pushBack</code>, <code>getMin</code>/<code>getMax</code> = <code>front</code>/<code>back</code>, <code>extractMin</code>/<code>extractMax</code> = <code>popFront</code>/<code>popBack</code>. Every operation is one deque operation.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>The deque stays sorted because insertMin/insertMax only ever add at the correct end</figcaption><pre><code>insertMin(5)      [5]\ninsertMax(10)     [5, 10]\ninsertMin(3)      [3, 5, 10]          ← front is always the min\ninsertMax(12)     [3, 5, 10, 12]      ← back is always the max\nextractMin() → 3  [5, 10, 12]\nextractMax() → 12 [5, 10]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from collections import deque\n\nclass MinMaxStructure:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.dq = deque()            # kept sorted: front = min, back = max\n\n    def insert_min(self, x):         # contract: x &lt;= current min\n        self.dq.appendleft(x)\n\n    def insert_max(self, x):         # contract: x &gt;= current max\n        self.dq.append(x)\n\n    def get_min(self):\n        return self.dq[0]\n\n    def get_max(self):\n        return self.dq[-1]\n\n    def extract_min(self):\n        return self.dq.popleft()\n\n    def extract_max(self):\n        return self.dq.pop()\n\ns = MinMaxStructure()\ns.insert_min(5)\ns.insert_max(10)\ns.insert_min(3)\ns.insert_max(12)\nprint(\"getMin:\", s.get_min())\nprint(\"getMax:\", s.get_max())\nprint(\"extractMin:\", s.extract_min())\nprint(\"getMin:\", s.get_min())\nprint(\"extractMax:\", s.extract_max())\nprint(\"getMax:\", s.get_max())</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;deque&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nclass MinMaxStructure {\n    deque&lt;int&gt; dq;                   // kept sorted: front = min, back = max\npublic:\n    void insertMin(int x) { dq.push_front(x); }   // contract: x &lt;= current min\n    void insertMax(int x) { dq.push_back(x); }    // contract: x &gt;= current max\n    int getMin() const { return dq.front(); }\n    int getMax() const { return dq.back(); }\n    int extractMin() { int x = dq.front(); dq.pop_front(); return x; }\n    int extractMax() { int x = dq.back(); dq.pop_back(); return x; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    MinMaxStructure s;\n    s.insertMin(5);\n    s.insertMax(10);\n    s.insertMin(3);\n    s.insertMax(12);\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"getMin: \" &lt;&lt; s.getMin() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"getMax: \" &lt;&lt; s.getMax() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"extractMin: \" &lt;&lt; s.extractMin() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"getMin: \" &lt;&lt; s.getMin() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"extractMax: \" &lt;&lt; s.extractMax() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"getMax: \" &lt;&lt; s.getMax() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.Deque;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static class MinMaxStructure {\n        Deque&lt;Integer&gt; dq = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();     // kept sorted: front = min, back = max\n        void insertMin(int x) { dq.addFirst(x); }   // contract: x &lt;= current min\n        void insertMax(int x) { dq.addLast(x); }    // contract: x &gt;= current max\n        int getMin() { return dq.peekFirst(); }\n        int getMax() { return dq.peekLast(); }\n        int extractMin() { return dq.pollFirst(); }\n        int extractMax() { return dq.pollLast(); }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        MinMaxStructure s = new MinMaxStructure();\n        s.insertMin(5);\n        s.insertMax(10);\n        s.insertMin(3);\n        s.insertMax(12);\n        System.out.println(\"getMin: \" + s.getMin());\n        System.out.println(\"getMax: \" + s.getMax());\n        System.out.println(\"extractMin: \" + s.extractMin());\n        System.out.println(\"getMin: \" + s.getMin());\n        System.out.println(\"extractMax: \" + s.extractMax());\n        System.out.println(\"getMax: \" + s.getMax());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Deque {                        // O(1) at both ends: indices grow outwards from 0\n    constructor() { this.items = {}; this.head = 0; this.tail = 0; }   // tail = one past the last\n    pushFront(x) { this.items[--this.head] = x; }\n    pushBack(x) { this.items[this.tail++] = x; }\n    popFront() { const x = this.items[this.head]; delete this.items[this.head++]; return x; }\n    popBack() { const x = this.items[--this.tail]; delete this.items[this.tail]; return x; }\n    front() { return this.items[this.head]; }\n    back() { return this.items[this.tail - 1]; }\n    get size() { return this.tail - this.head; }\n}\n\nclass MinMaxStructure {\n    constructor() { this.dq = new Deque(); }       // kept sorted: front = min, back = max\n    insertMin(x) { this.dq.pushFront(x); }         // contract: x &lt;= current min\n    insertMax(x) { this.dq.pushBack(x); }          // contract: x &gt;= current max\n    getMin() { return this.dq.front(); }\n    getMax() { return this.dq.back(); }\n    extractMin() { return this.dq.popFront(); }\n    extractMax() { return this.dq.popBack(); }\n}\n\nconst s = new MinMaxStructure();\ns.insertMin(5);\ns.insertMax(10);\ns.insertMin(3);\ns.insertMax(12);\nconsole.log(\"getMin:\", s.getMin());\nconsole.log(\"getMax:\", s.getMax());\nconsole.log(\"extractMin:\", s.extractMin());\nconsole.log(\"getMin:\", s.getMin());\nconsole.log(\"extractMax:\", s.extractMax());\nconsole.log(\"getMax:\", s.getMax());</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>getMin: 3\ngetMax: 12\nextractMin: 3\ngetMin: 5\nextractMax: 12\ngetMax: 10</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(1) per operation</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>State the contract out loud in an interview: this structure is correct <strong>only if</strong> <code>insertMin</code> is always given a value no larger than the current minimum and <code>insertMax</code> a value no smaller than the current maximum (the original notes called it a 'MinMaxStack' and did not say so). Insert <code>7</code> into <code>[3, 5, 10, 12]</code> through either method and the sorted order — and every later answer — is wrong. For arbitrary inserts you need a min-max heap or two heaps (<code>O(log n)</code>).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-chart-bar\"></i>Maximums of all subarrays of size k (sliding window maximum)</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>arr = [10, 8, 5, 12, 15, 7, 6], k = 3</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>10 12 15 15 15</code></p></div>\n<p>For every window of <code>k</code> consecutive elements, print its maximum. The windows here are <code>[10,8,5]</code>, <code>[8,5,12]</code>, <code>[5,12,15]</code>, <code>[12,15,7]</code>, <code>[15,7,6]</code>.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Naive — O(n·k): recompute the maximum of every window</figcaption><pre><code>def window_max_naive(arr, k):\n    res = []\n    for i in range(len(arr) - k + 1):\n        res.append(max(arr[i:i + k]))    # rescan each window\n    return res\n\nprint(*window_max_naive([10, 8, 5, 12, 15, 7, 6], 3))</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>10 12 15 15 15</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Keep a deque of <strong>indices</strong> whose values are <strong>decreasing</strong> from front to back — the monotonic-stack idea from the Stack topic, with one extra operation. When <code>arr[i]</code> arrives, pop from the <strong>back</strong> every index whose value is <code>&lt;= arr[i]</code> (they can never be a window maximum again: <code>arr[i]</code> is bigger and will outlive them). Push <code>i</code> at the back. Pop from the <strong>front</strong> the index that has slid out of the window (<code>front &lt;= i − k</code>). The front is now the maximum of the current window. The deque is the set of elements that are still <em>useful</em> — each is the maximum of the suffix of the window that starts at it.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Front of the deque = maximum of the current window; every index enters and leaves at most once</figcaption><pre><code>arr = [10, 8, 5, 12, 15, 7, 6], k = 3        deque holds indices; shown as values\n\ni  arr[i]  pop back while &lt;= arr[i]   push   drop front if out of window   deque (values)   window max\n0   10     –                          0      –                             [10]             (window not full)\n1    8     –                          1      –                             [10, 8]          (window not full)\n2    5     –                          2      –                             [10, 8, 5]       10\n3   12     5, 8, 10                   3      –                             [12]             12\n4   15     12                         4      –                             [15]             15\n5    7     –                          5      –                             [15, 7]          15\n6    6     –                          6      index 4 still inside          [15, 7, 6]       15</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from collections import deque\n\ndef window_max(arr, k):\n    dq = deque()                 # indices; arr values decreasing front → back\n    res = []\n    for i in range(len(arr)):\n        while dq and arr[dq[-1]] &lt;= arr[i]:   # smaller-or-equal older elements are useless now\n            dq.pop()\n        dq.append(i)\n        if dq[0] &lt;= i - k:                    # front has slid out of the window\n            dq.popleft()\n        if i &gt;= k - 1:                        # window is full: front is its maximum\n            res.append(arr[dq[0]])\n    return res\n\nprint(*window_max([10, 8, 5, 12, 15, 7, 6], 3))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;deque&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nvector&lt;int&gt; windowMax(const vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; arr, int k) {\n    deque&lt;int&gt; dq;               // indices; arr values decreasing front → back\n    vector&lt;int&gt; res;\n    int n = arr.size();\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) {\n        while (!dq.empty() &amp;&amp; arr[dq.back()] &lt;= arr[i]) dq.pop_back();   // useless now\n        dq.push_back(i);\n        if (dq.front() &lt;= i - k) dq.pop_front();   // front has slid out of the window\n        if (i &gt;= k - 1) res.push_back(arr[dq.front()]);   // window is full: front is its max\n    }\n    return res;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    for (int x : windowMax({10, 8, 5, 12, 15, 7, 6}, 3)) cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; \" \";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.Deque;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static int[] windowMax(int[] arr, int k) {\n        Deque&lt;Integer&gt; dq = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();   // indices; arr values decreasing front → back\n        int n = arr.length;\n        int[] res = new int[n - k + 1];\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) {\n            while (!dq.isEmpty() &amp;&amp; arr[dq.peekLast()] &lt;= arr[i]) dq.pollLast();   // useless now\n            dq.addLast(i);\n            if (dq.peekFirst() &lt;= i - k) dq.pollFirst();   // front has slid out of the window\n            if (i &gt;= k - 1) res[i - k + 1] = arr[dq.peekFirst()];   // window full: front is its max\n        }\n        return res;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (int x : windowMax(new int[]{10, 8, 5, 12, 15, 7, 6}, 3)) sb.append(x).append(' ');\n        System.out.println(sb.toString().trim());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function windowMax(arr, k) {\n    const dq = [];               // indices; arr values decreasing front → back\n    let head = 0;                // dq[head] is the front — no O(n) shift()\n    const res = [];\n    for (let i = 0; i &lt; arr.length; i++) {\n        while (dq.length &gt; head &amp;&amp; arr[dq[dq.length - 1]] &lt;= arr[i]) dq.pop();   // useless now\n        dq.push(i);\n        if (dq[head] &lt;= i - k) head++;                 // front has slid out of the window\n        if (i &gt;= k - 1) res.push(arr[dq[head]]);       // window is full: front is its maximum\n    }\n    return res;\n}\n\nconsole.log(windowMax([10, 8, 5, 12, 15, 7, 6], 3).join(\" \"));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>10 12 15 15 15</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n) — each index is pushed and popped at most once</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(k) for the deque</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>Store <strong>indices</strong>, not values — you need the index to know when the front has left the window. And pop from the back on <code>&lt;=</code>, not <code>&lt;</code>: an older <em>equal</em> element leaves the window earlier than the new one, so keeping it would only make the deque longer. The first <code>k − 1</code> iterations do not emit anything; the original notes handled that with a separate warm-up loop, the version above folds it into one loop with the <code>i &gt;= k − 1</code> test.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Variants</div><p>The same deque gives the <strong>sliding window minimum</strong> by flipping the comparison to <code>&gt;=</code>. It is also the engine behind <code>O(n)</code> solutions for <em>shortest subarray with sum at least k</em> and the monotonic-queue optimisation of some DP recurrences.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-route\"></i>First circular tour (petrol pumps)</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>petrol = [4, 8, 7, 4], dist = [6, 5, 3, 5] (pumps numbered from 1)</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>2</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>petrol = [1, 2, 3], dist = [3, 3, 3]</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>-1</code></p></div>\n<p><code>n</code> petrol pumps sit on a circle. Pump <code>i</code> offers <code>petrol[i]</code> litres, and <code>dist[i]</code> litres are burned driving from pump <code>i</code> to pump <code>i + 1</code>. Starting with an empty tank, find the first pump from which a truck can complete the full circle, or report that none exists. Here the net gain at each pump is <code>-2, 3, 4, -1</code>; starting at pump 2 the tank reads <code>3 → 7 → 6 → 4</code>, never negative.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Naive — O(n²): simulate the circle from every start</figcaption><pre><code>def first_tour_naive(petrol, dist):\n    n = len(petrol)\n    for start in range(n):             # try every start …\n        fuel, i = 0, start\n        while True:                    # … and simulate the whole circle\n            fuel += petrol[i] - dist[i]\n            if fuel &lt; 0:\n                break\n            i = (i + 1) % n\n            if i == start:\n                return start + 1\n    return -1\n\nprint(first_tour_naive([4, 8, 7, 4], [6, 5, 3, 5]))\nprint(first_tour_naive([1, 2, 3], [3, 3, 3]))</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>2\n-1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Think of the visited pumps as a <strong>queue</strong>. If the tank goes negative on reaching pump <code>i</code> after starting at <code>s</code>, then every start between <code>s</code> and <code>i</code> fails too: the tank was non-negative on arrival at each of them, so starting there with an <em>empty</em> tank only makes things worse. Dequeue them all at once — the next candidate start is <code>i + 1</code>. Each pump is enqueued (visited as part of a tour) and dequeued (ruled out) at most once, so one pass suffices. Finally, the candidate that survives to the end is valid only if the <strong>total</strong> surplus (what it gains after its start plus the deficit of the skipped prefix it still has to cross) is <code>&gt;= 0</code>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Scan once</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Keep <code>start = 0</code>, <code>fuel = 0</code> (fuel since <code>start</code>) and <code>deficit = 0</code> (total shortfall of all ruled-out prefixes).</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">On a negative tank at pump i</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Everything from <code>start</code> to <code>i</code> is ruled out: <code>deficit += fuel</code>, <code>start = i + 1</code>, <code>fuel = 0</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">At the end</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>start</code> completes the circle only if <code>fuel + deficit &gt;= 0</code> — the surplus it collects from <code>start</code> to <code>n − 1</code> must cover the deficit of the wrapped-around prefix. Otherwise no pump works (equivalently, <code>sum(petrol) &lt; sum(dist)</code>).</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def first_tour(petrol, dist):\n    start, fuel, deficit = 0, 0, 0\n    for i in range(len(petrol)):\n        fuel += petrol[i] - dist[i]\n        if fuel &lt; 0:                 # pumps start..i are all ruled out at once\n            deficit += fuel          # remember how short that stretch was\n            start = i + 1\n            fuel = 0\n    return start + 1 if fuel + deficit &gt;= 0 else -1   # 1-based pump number\n\nprint(first_tour([4, 8, 7, 4], [6, 5, 3, 5]))\nprint(first_tour([1, 2, 3], [3, 3, 3]))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nint firstTour(const vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; petrol, const vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; dist) {\n    int start = 0, fuel = 0, deficit = 0;\n    int n = petrol.size();\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) {\n        fuel += petrol[i] - dist[i];\n        if (fuel &lt; 0) {              // pumps start..i are all ruled out at once\n            deficit += fuel;         // remember how short that stretch was\n            start = i + 1;\n            fuel = 0;\n        }\n    }\n    return fuel + deficit &gt;= 0 ? start + 1 : -1;   // 1-based pump number\n}\n\nint main() {\n    cout &lt;&lt; firstTour({4, 8, 7, 4}, {6, 5, 3, 5}) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; firstTour({1, 2, 3}, {3, 3, 3}) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static int firstTour(int[] petrol, int[] dist) {\n        int start = 0, fuel = 0, deficit = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; petrol.length; i++) {\n            fuel += petrol[i] - dist[i];\n            if (fuel &lt; 0) {              // pumps start..i are all ruled out at once\n                deficit += fuel;         // remember how short that stretch was\n                start = i + 1;\n                fuel = 0;\n            }\n        }\n        return fuel + deficit &gt;= 0 ? start + 1 : -1;   // 1-based pump number\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(firstTour(new int[]{4, 8, 7, 4}, new int[]{6, 5, 3, 5}));\n        System.out.println(firstTour(new int[]{1, 2, 3}, new int[]{3, 3, 3}));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function firstTour(petrol, dist) {\n    let start = 0, fuel = 0, deficit = 0;\n    for (let i = 0; i &lt; petrol.length; i++) {\n        fuel += petrol[i] - dist[i];\n        if (fuel &lt; 0) {                  // pumps start..i are all ruled out at once\n            deficit += fuel;             // remember how short that stretch was\n            start = i + 1;\n            fuel = 0;\n        }\n    }\n    return fuel + deficit &gt;= 0 ? start + 1 : -1;   // 1-based pump number\n}\n\nconsole.log(firstTour([4, 8, 7, 4], [6, 5, 3, 5]));\nconsole.log(firstTour([1, 2, 3], [3, 3, 3]));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>2\n-1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><strong>Why the final check is enough.</strong> If <code>sum(petrol) &gt;= sum(dist)</code> a tour always exists, and the scan above finds the <em>first</em> one: every start before <code>start</code> was explicitly ruled out, and <code>start</code> itself never ran dry on the stretch to the end; wrapping around, it meets the ruled-out prefixes with <code>fuel</code> in the tank, and <code>fuel + deficit &gt;= 0</code> says that is enough. The explicit queue version (two indices <code>start</code>, <code>end</code> walking around the circle, dequeuing from the front while the tank is negative) is the same argument spelled out with <code>(end + 1) % n</code> arithmetic.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>The answer is the pump <strong>number</strong>, and the original notes count from 1 (<code>start + 1</code>); many online judges count from 0. Say which convention you use. Also do not forget the <code>-1</code> case — with <code>start</code> ending at <code>n</code> (every pump ruled out) the <code>start + 1</code> formula would otherwise return a nonsense <code>n + 1</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-message-question\"></i>Interview Q&amp;A</h3>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Queue vs deque vs priority queue — when do I use which?</summary><div><p>A queue when the order of processing is the order of arrival (BFS, task scheduling). A deque when you also need the other end: sliding windows, work-stealing, palindromes, undo/redo with a bounded history. A priority queue when the next element is determined by a key, not by position — it costs <code>O(log n)</code> per operation instead of <code>O(1)</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is the sliding window maximum O(n) if there is a while loop inside the for loop?</summary><div><p>Every index is appended once and removed at most once (from the back by a bigger element, or from the front by sliding out). Total deque operations are at most <code>2n</code>, so the amortised cost per element is constant — the same argument as for the monotonic stack.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How would you implement a deque yourself?</summary><div><p>A circular array with <code>head</code> and <code>tail</code> indices (and either a <code>size</code> counter or one wasted slot to tell full from empty) gives <code>O(1)</code> at both ends with cache-friendly storage; grow by doubling when full. A doubly linked list also works but spends a node per element. <code>collections.deque</code> uses blocks of 64 pointers in a doubly linked list of blocks; <code>ArrayDeque</code> is a circular array.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How would you generate the numbers in the digit problem without a queue?</summary><div><p>Treat the index as a counter in base <code>k</code> (the number of digits) with a bijective numeration: the <code>m</code>-th number's digits are the base-<code>k</code> representation of <code>m</code> with digits mapped through the sorted digit set. That is <code>O(log m)</code> per number and needs no memory — but the BFS version is the one interviewers expect first, because it generalises to any 'generate in order by extending' problem.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why does the petrol-pump argument rule out all pumps between the start and the failure point?</summary><div><p>Suppose the tour from <code>s</code> first goes negative on arriving at <code>i</code>. For any <code>s &lt; t &lt;= i</code>, the tank on arrival at <code>t</code> (from <code>s</code>) was non-negative, so starting at <code>t</code> with an empty tank gives at every later pump at most the fuel the <code>s</code>-tour had there — and that went negative at <code>i</code>. So <code>t</code> fails too, and the next possible start is <code>i + 1</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}